  "step_3": "The decision reason requires evaluating the skewness of the lotteries, specifically whether one is more right-skewed than the other. The individual did not consider skewness, nor did they refer to the distribution of probabilities across outcomes, the presence of extreme low outcomes, or the relative weighting of favorable vs. unfavorable outcomes. Instead, the individual focused on the certainty of the outcome and the small difference in payoffs. The justification is based on risk aversion and perceived safety, not on the statistical shape of the distribution. Therefore, there is no evidence that the individual applied the skewness-based decision rule. The information considered by the individual (certainty, small payoff difference) does not align with the information required by the reason (skewness, probability distribution across outcomes). The reasoning process also differs fundamentally: the individual avoided risk due to certainty, whereas the reason would prefer a lottery with more probability mass on lower outcomes (right-skewed), which is not the case here. Thus, the decision reason was not used.",
